# preserve.md

*Mixed document for the aligner: several real GFM tables below must be re-aligned so the `|` borders line up in a monospace renderer, while every non-table construct in this file passes through byte-identical.*

## 1. First table — deliberately sloppy spacing

English prose introducing the first table. Cell padding here is a mess on purpose: the aligner must re-space every cell while leaving the surrounding prose alone.

| left             | center   | right   |
| :--------------- | :------: | ------: |
| a                | b        | c       |
| much longer cell | x        | y       |
| z                | w        | v       |

*The table above must be aligned; the italic note and the headings around it must not move.*

## 2. Idempotency target — already perfect

A table that is already aligned exactly to the single-space-padding convention, with every cell — including the delimiter — padded to the column width. A correct aligner must leave it byte-identical, so repeated runs cause no churn.

| name    | qty  |
| :------ | ---: |
| apples  | 3    |
| bananas | 12   |

## 3. English prose, then a Hungarian section

Some English filler so the tables are always surrounded by real prose, then a Hungarian paragraph. The accented characters and the sentences must survive alignment untouched.

Itt egy magyar bekezdés, amely a táblázatok között szerepel: az ékezetes betűknek érintetlenül kell maradniuk, miközben a környező táblázatok igazodnak.

| Név        | Érték      | Megjegyzés     |
| :--------- | ---------: | :------------- |
| alma       | 12         | gyümölcs       |
| körte      | 4          | édes is        |
| őszibarack | 3          | nyári gyümölcs |

*Hungarian table: the display widths of á, é, í, ó, ö, ő, ú, ű must each be counted as 1.*

## 4. Table flush against a heading and a paragraph

No blank line separates the heading from the table, nor the table from the paragraph below it. All three constructs are valid markdown and must survive apart from cell padding.

### Measurements
| metric     | value   |
| :--------- | ------: |
| latency    | 12ms    |
| throughput | 900     |
This paragraph starts immediately after the last table row; it has no pipe and must stay a paragraph.

## 5. Mixed pipe styles within one table

Some rows keep both border pipes, some drop them — one logical table must be re-rendered consistently.

| one   | two   |
| ----- | ----- |
| three | four  |
| five  | six   |
| seven | eight |

*All four body rows belong to the same table; the aligner must treat them as one unit.*

## 6. Trailing spaces on some rows

Some rows end with spaces after the final pipe (rows 1 and 3). The aligner must make one deliberate, consistent choice for trailing whitespace.

| a | b |   
| c | d |
| e | f |  

## 7. Hand-made wrapped row

One logical row was wrapped by hand: the continuation line starts with an empty first cell. The aligner must re-parse and re-render this table correctly — the continuation belongs to the previous row, not a row of its own.

| col1   | col2                                     | col3   |
| :----- | :--------------------------------------- | :----- |
| rowA   | this is a longish cell that someone      |        |
|        | wrapped                                  | tail   |
|        | by hand onto a second physical line      |        |
| rowB   | short                                    | end    |

*If the aligner re-wraps this table the join must be lossless; the two physical lines must be handled as one logical row.*

## 8. Escaped pipes and inline-code pipes are cell content

Escaped pipes and pipes inside inline code spans are content, not column separators; alignment must count them as cell text.

| pattern   | note             |
| --------- | ---------------- |
| a\|b      | escaped pipe     |
| `x|y`     | inline code pipe |
| c         | plain            |

## 9. A table between two code fences

The fences around the table are code and must stay byte-identical while the table between them is aligned.

```text
| code | table |
| ---- | ----- |
```

| data   | goes   | here   |
| :----- | :----- | :----- |
| one    | two    | three  |

```text
| more | code |
```

## 10. Blockquote table and a quoted fence

The script supports `> ` blockquote prefixes: the quoted table below is alignable. The quoted fence after it is code and must not move.

> | quoted   | table   |
> | :------- | ------: |
> | a        | 1       |

> ```
> | quote | code |
> ```

## 11. Final mixed section

One last table followed by blockquotes, a list, a thematic break, a setext heading, an HTML comment and indented code — none of those may change.

| last   | table   |
| ------ | ------: |
| x      | 1       |

> A plain blockquote that must not be touched.

- a list item
- another list item

---

A setext heading
================

<!-- comment with | pipe -->

    indented code with | pipe

English closing paragraph: the file ends here with a table, prose, and untouched blocks all intact.
